<h2>Login (Secure)</h2>

<div
  style="
    border: 2px solid #0a0;
    background-color: #e8ffe8;
    padding: 10px 15px;
    max-width: 480px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
  "
>
  <strong>Secure version:</strong>
  <p>
    On this branch the login form is now secured. On the backend I use a
    parameterised SQL query for the email lookup and bcrypt-hashed passwords,
    so the email and password fields can no longer be abused for SQL injection.
  </p>

  <p>Seeded Test Account for the secure branch:</p>
  <pre>
Email: conor@test.com
Password: Pass123
  </pre>
</div>

<% if (error) { %>
  <!-- I show a simple error message here if the login fails -->
  <p style="color: red;"><%= error %></p>
<% } %>

<form method="post" action="/login">
  <label>Email</label><br />
  <input name="email" type="text" /><br /><br />

  <label>Password</label><br />
  <input name="password" type="password" /><br /><br />

  <button type="submit">Login</button>
</form>

<hr />

<h3>SQL Injection Payload (now blocked on the secure branch)</h3>
<p>
  In the insecure branch this payload in the email field would log you in
  without a valid password because the query string was concatenated:
</p>
<code>' OR 1=1 --</code>
<p>
  On this secure branch it is treated as data only, because I now use a
  parameterised SQL query instead of string concatenation.
</p>
